  <section aria-labelledby="bio">
    <h2 id="bio">Biography</h2>
    <figure class="portrait">
      <picture>
        <source srcset="/assets/img/portrait.avif" type="image/avif">
        <img src="/assets/img/portrait.webp" width="640" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async"
             alt="Salvatore Di Dio, smiling, in a dark jacket and white shirt against a light grey wall.">
      </picture>
      <figcaption>Photo © PUSH — Mauro Filippi</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Salvatore Di Dio is Associate Professor of Design at the University of Palermo. His research
       addresses a precise problem: the distance between what people say they want and what they
       choose every morning. They recognise the climate emergency, then they take the car.</p>
    <p>He has worked on that gap since 2012, when he coordinated trafficO2 (€1.6M, of which €1.25M
       from the Italian Ministry of Education and Research). It led to the Horizon 2020 project
       MUV2020 — €3.9M, 14 partners, 8 countries, part of CIVITAS — where he served as Project
       Coordinator. That research produced MUV B Corp, a University of Palermo academic spin-off he
       co-founded and where he is Scientific Director: a platform that reframes sustainable mobility
       as a competitive sport, with citizens as athletes, city governments as coaches and companies
       as sponsors. The approach does not depend on local infrastructure and yields a 33.8% average
       reduction in CO<sub>2</sub> across over 25 cities, measured with an algorithm certified by
       RINA to ISO 14064-II.</p>
    <p>He is scientific lead of B-CIRCULAR, on circular economy from textile waste, and sits on the
       University of Palermo's Steering Committee on Artificial Intelligence. Since February 2026 he
       has been a member of the ADI Permanent Observatory, assessing projects for the ADI Design
       Index. In 2013 he co-founded PUSH, a non-profit design lab that has managed over €5M in
       applied research funding and convened six editions of the Urban Thinkers Campus with
       UN-Habitat, and The Piranesi Experience, a documentary production company selected at the
       Tribeca Film Festival and the International Film Festival Rotterdam.</p>
    <p>He has published over 50 scientific contributions, has spoken twice at TEDx and is an Aspen
       Junior Fellow Alumnus. He earned his PhD in energy and environmental applied physics from the
       University of Palermo in 2015, with a research period at MIT.</p>
  </section>

